1
00:00:19,943 --> 00:00:23,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these are some questions that come from all around the world.

2
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:32,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'll start with this first one, and it's in terms of persistence in practice.

3
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:40,887
[SPEAKER_00]: And a friend asks, how important is having a fixed routine?

4
00:00:43,604 --> 00:00:46,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it have a big impact on dhamma progress?

5
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:52,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Producing wholesome actions, mental qualities, or it doesn't really matter much?

6
00:00:53,509 --> 00:00:59,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Should you have persistence, or can you just like... Well, I think, yeah, yeah, yeah.

7
00:00:59,832 --> 00:01:03,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it depends on every individual.

8
00:01:05,716 --> 00:01:05,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

9
00:01:07,243 --> 00:01:24,038
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a person with a... with tendencies of complete kind of disorganization, laziness, cannot adhere to basic worldly routine or discipline, then, yeah, it's very helpful.

10
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:31,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to, you know, get your mind in a certain sense of responsibility and stick into the... stick into the training.

11
00:01:32,943 --> 00:01:53,916
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you have the other type of an individual that would be pretty much holding the view that training, routine, being methodical, precise, is the training, is the development of dhamma, which it isn't.

12
00:01:54,696 --> 00:01:55,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an external form.

13
00:01:57,498 --> 00:01:59,119
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to see which individual you are,

14
00:02:00,407 --> 00:02:04,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you are the former, then, yeah, get some routine to keep you in check.

15
00:02:05,131 --> 00:02:17,459
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're the latter, then maybe, you know, become more, kind of, less routine-based and experience that, you know, mind sort of floating away without having anything fixed to do or to adhere to.

16
00:02:19,421 --> 00:02:21,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Because either way, that's not the dhamma.

17
00:02:21,462 --> 00:02:22,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

18
00:02:23,958 --> 00:02:30,084
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the reflections you should be making on the level of practice of dhamma, the accomplishment in virtue, that's not a method, that's not a routine.

19
00:02:30,104 --> 00:02:34,227
[SPEAKER_01]: That comes with insight, and repetition, and the training, and sustaining the effort.

20
00:02:34,828 --> 00:02:37,731
[SPEAKER_01]: If the routine helps you for that, you do it.

21
00:02:38,231 --> 00:02:41,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But sometimes the routine can take place instead of that.

22
00:02:41,895 --> 00:02:44,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And all I have to do is the routine and I'm practicing the dhamma.

23
00:02:45,358 --> 00:02:47,540
[SPEAKER_01]: I just get up at this, do this, do this, do this.

24
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,881
[SPEAKER_01]: I finish the day.

25
00:02:49,421 --> 00:02:52,684
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a successful day because I've successfully completed my routine.

26
00:02:53,405 --> 00:02:55,567
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not how the progress in dhamma is measured.

27
00:02:57,228 --> 00:02:59,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and you might be kind of in between.

28
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:10,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start with the routine, but then keep yourself in check in case you start taking routine in that kind of mechanical sense whereby, as I said,

29
00:03:11,892 --> 00:03:16,857
[SPEAKER_01]: I just need to go through routine and then I'll be anxiety-free and worry-free because I completed my duty.

30
00:03:18,218 --> 00:03:21,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're taking it as a duty, it's not the dhamma, it's not the virtue anymore.

31
00:03:21,522 --> 00:03:22,843
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the external projection of it.

32
00:03:24,849 --> 00:03:28,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can become like a... a robot, you know.

33
00:03:29,092 --> 00:03:30,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unaware of what's actually going on.

34
00:03:30,513 --> 00:03:31,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

35
00:03:31,114 --> 00:03:32,515
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the risk of it, always.

36
00:03:32,895 --> 00:03:35,597
[SPEAKER_01]: But sometimes you can be too loose and sometimes you can be too tight.

37
00:03:36,157 --> 00:03:36,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can't just always...

38
00:03:36,938 --> 00:03:44,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, routines are always good, because somebody might be already too tight and too externally projected on the sense of duty and observances and stuff that they do.

39
00:03:46,044 --> 00:03:54,632
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, no, they need to loosen up, actually, these routines, to get that sense of, whoa, my mind, my responsibility, my choice, my intention.

40
00:03:54,812 --> 00:04:02,058
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you have the other individual who's too loose, which is absolutely, and equally, no sense of responsibility or intentions, because it's too loose.

41
00:04:02,818 --> 00:04:06,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing's taken internally, taken on.

42
00:04:06,221 --> 00:04:08,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, yeah, they would need some external

43
00:04:09,317 --> 00:04:16,140
[SPEAKER_01]: adherence to the certain, you know, routines and principles, just to, you know, get them a bit into shape.

44
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:25,924
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, in terms of persistence, you know, just you persistently, for example, keeping the precepts.

45
00:04:28,205 --> 00:04:28,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

46
00:04:28,926 --> 00:04:33,027
[SPEAKER_00]: You just, you have to build it up.

47
00:04:33,408 --> 00:04:34,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, exactly.

48
00:04:34,368 --> 00:04:38,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people can take, people can take persistence to mean, I'll just, you know,

49
00:04:39,632 --> 00:04:41,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Adhere to your determination and persist.

50
00:04:41,793 --> 00:04:43,354
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one way of persistence, sure.

51
00:04:43,954 --> 00:04:45,295
[SPEAKER_01]: But persistence is exactly that.

52
00:04:45,575 --> 00:04:49,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it can be much more relaxed, yet still persistent.

53
00:04:50,397 --> 00:04:53,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't have specific determination to adhere, to do.

54
00:04:53,938 --> 00:04:56,560
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, you don't do certain actions.

55
00:04:56,620 --> 00:04:58,360
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not going against the precepts.

56
00:04:58,560 --> 00:04:59,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are persistent.

57
00:05:00,381 --> 00:05:06,264
[SPEAKER_01]: You are persistently maintaining and protecting the basis of your virtue, that in return protects the basis of your mind.

58
00:05:07,834 --> 00:05:14,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes if you feel you're getting looser, maybe you need to build up some determination or something, but not as a rule of thumb.

59
00:05:14,457 --> 00:05:18,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I always must have this persisting determination and focus.

60
00:05:19,719 --> 00:05:23,781
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because sometimes that can be the reason for you not seeing where the practice is.

61
00:05:23,901 --> 00:05:24,621
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too tight.

62
00:05:25,937 --> 00:05:37,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, often, you know, the simile that the Buddha gave, the the the lute strings, like you want to play the music with them, but you will not be able to play the music if the strings are too tight or too loose.

63
00:05:38,321 --> 00:05:39,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to find the right balance.

64
00:05:42,882 --> 00:05:54,326
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are one who is really trying, at least, you're really trying and you're persisting and you feel there is some

65
00:05:54,935 --> 00:06:00,858
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, development, at least, you know, calmness and dispassion.

66
00:06:01,478 --> 00:06:07,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you fail, you make a mistake, you break a precept.

67
00:06:08,102 --> 00:06:15,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you just, you know, it feels like all your work is kind of lost, sometimes.

68
00:06:17,466 --> 00:06:18,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that the case, you think?

69
00:06:19,694 --> 00:06:21,355
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the attitude then, for that person?

70
00:06:21,395 --> 00:06:22,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can really feel like, this is it.

71
00:06:22,855 --> 00:06:25,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, now the mind tries to go the other way, exactly.

72
00:06:25,476 --> 00:06:26,536
[SPEAKER_01]: I've really messed up now.

73
00:06:26,636 --> 00:06:27,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

74
00:06:27,577 --> 00:06:28,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends.

75
00:06:29,998 --> 00:06:35,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Depends what precept you broke, to what extent, how much, and so on.

76
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:40,521
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's like those serious precepts of killing or something like that, then, yeah, maybe you have messed up.

77
00:06:42,382 --> 00:06:49,004
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's not on that level, then, yeah, you messed up, but not in a sense of that you cannot grow out of it now.

78
00:06:50,717 --> 00:06:52,438
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you messed up, sure.

79
00:06:53,058 --> 00:06:54,179
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize the mistake.

80
00:06:55,119 --> 00:06:59,282
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, you basically be even more aware next time.

81
00:07:00,882 --> 00:07:01,983
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's good, actually.

82
00:07:02,884 --> 00:07:12,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I know the mind can fall into that trap of, like, negativity about, oh, I messed up, it's unworthless and all of that.

83
00:07:13,029 --> 00:07:16,731
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to endure it, because that's pretty much the result of you messing up.

84
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:22,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't just try to, like, oh, you know, I should not think these thoughts.

85
00:07:25,523 --> 00:07:40,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Because feeling bad about messing up, when you were actually intentionally messed up and broke the precepts, will, as I said, make you more mindful next time, if you don't try to negate that bad feeling and allow it to endure as long as it lasts.

86
00:07:41,825 --> 00:07:48,572
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, you're taking on more weight of your action of messing up, through enduring that pain, which is a result of you messing up.

87
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:59,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, eh... as you pick up your precepts again, when the...

88
00:08:00,539 --> 00:08:04,502
[SPEAKER_01]: When the challenges come, well, the memory of pain will be fresh.

89
00:08:05,083 --> 00:08:08,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain of how it was when I chose to break the precepts.

90
00:08:09,006 --> 00:08:11,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you will not necessarily do it so easily.

91
00:08:12,950 --> 00:08:13,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain of regret.

92
00:08:14,031 --> 00:08:14,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

93
00:08:14,931 --> 00:08:15,412
[SPEAKER_01]: The remorse.

94
00:08:15,872 --> 00:08:16,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Remorse.

95
00:08:16,753 --> 00:08:18,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Remorse, guilt, can be very useful.

96
00:08:19,415 --> 00:08:24,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, can be irrational, in which case you have to be aware of that as well, and not unnecessarily fuel it.

97
00:08:25,919 --> 00:08:26,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I done anything wrong?

98
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:27,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

99
00:08:27,441 --> 00:08:27,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

100
00:08:27,781 --> 00:08:30,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, then there is a base for guilt, and maybe I should endure it a bit.

101
00:08:31,266 --> 00:08:33,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes you just feel guilty, but have I done it wrong?

102
00:08:33,288 --> 00:08:33,448
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

103
00:08:33,608 --> 00:08:36,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now it's irrational, and now I shall not feel it.

104
00:08:36,992 --> 00:08:38,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you probably have to endure it.

105
00:08:39,856 --> 00:08:40,437
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's that...

106
00:08:42,926 --> 00:08:50,169
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, okay, that inner voice, there was that one question, what should you do about that inner critic?

107
00:08:50,189 --> 00:08:51,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Inner critic, yeah, yeah, yeah.

108
00:08:51,489 --> 00:08:53,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Inner voice, I think that... That actually goes exactly there.

109
00:08:54,371 --> 00:08:57,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as I said... You messed up, you messed up real bad.

110
00:08:57,432 --> 00:08:57,992
[SPEAKER_00]: You messed up real bad.

111
00:08:58,012 --> 00:08:58,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You're such an idiot.

112
00:08:58,732 --> 00:08:59,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever it is.

113
00:08:59,433 --> 00:09:00,833
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll never make it.

114
00:09:00,933 --> 00:09:01,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

115
00:09:02,554 --> 00:09:12,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as painful as that is, you need to recognize that pretty much all of those mental states, inner critic, sense of guilt, whatever else, your mind is revolving around,

116
00:09:14,194 --> 00:09:15,634
[SPEAKER_01]: are the symptoms of the problem.

117
00:09:16,775 --> 00:09:17,395
[SPEAKER_01]: They're the results.

118
00:09:19,176 --> 00:09:23,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to just see them as a problem and then, oh, how do I stop the inner critic?

119
00:09:24,857 --> 00:09:30,279
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you actually see inner critic as an indicator of a problem that you're not seeing.

120
00:09:31,919 --> 00:09:40,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the the power that that inner critic, that your own mind has, is the power you've been sort of feeding it through certain other actions that you are not aware of.

121
00:09:42,127 --> 00:09:55,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, when like people have this irrational guilt constantly, or irrational critic, or something, it's the symptom of basically, when those things are not present, of your actions, of your actions and choices.

122
00:09:56,783 --> 00:10:00,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you endured in a critic, don't act out of it.

123
00:10:01,712 --> 00:10:02,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't feel it.

124
00:10:02,192 --> 00:10:03,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't argue with it either.

125
00:10:04,694 --> 00:10:13,000
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when inner critic goes away, then you keep an eye on your choices, and things you do, and things you think, and things you allow your mind to revolve around.

126
00:10:14,021 --> 00:10:17,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how you're providing fuel for the inner critic later on.

127
00:10:18,404 --> 00:10:28,211
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, when inner critic is not there, the lack of awareness in regard to wholesomeness of your choices, actions, thoughts, themes that you entertain,

128
00:10:29,252 --> 00:10:40,458
[SPEAKER_01]: The lack of awareness there, it's pretty much a way of undermining yourself, in the sense of, when the inner critic comes, you are completely at its mercy.

129
00:10:40,498 --> 00:10:43,500
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no... you can't say, no, stop it, to your own mind.

130
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to endure it, because you put yourself in that position.

131
00:10:49,613 --> 00:10:57,495
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can start to see the connection between your behavior, your choices, the mental themes throughout the day, things you do, things you say.

132
00:10:58,635 --> 00:11:07,116
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start seeing inner critic or that irrational guilt or whatever else your mind revolves around in these negative states, see that as a symptom.

133
00:11:08,137 --> 00:11:09,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't just try to get rid of that.

134
00:11:10,477 --> 00:11:14,698
[SPEAKER_01]: But say, no, no, no, this is the result of something else I do when this is not present.

135
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:19,822
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you start looking in that broader sense, the connection between the two will become apparent.

136
00:11:19,842 --> 00:11:20,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the main thing.

137
00:11:20,782 --> 00:11:21,942
[SPEAKER_01]: People don't see the connection.

138
00:11:22,663 --> 00:11:29,265
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't see the connection between day-to-day behavior and their own liability to suffer on account of their own mind.

139
00:11:30,586 --> 00:11:37,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for that, virtue, training, reflection, all that's required, repetitively.

140
00:11:37,048 --> 00:11:37,928
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep doing it.

141
00:11:38,588 --> 00:11:41,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And wisdom would be the result of it, when you start seeing the connection.

142
00:11:41,570 --> 00:11:42,630
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know for yourself.

143
00:11:44,194 --> 00:11:51,840
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to take it on faith that a good behavior, restrained speech, restrained mind, will result in the well-being of your mind.

144
00:11:52,580 --> 00:12:00,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of makes sense, but until you see the connection directly, you will be taking it on faith, which is fine, as long as it makes you make the right effort.

145
00:12:01,047 --> 00:12:04,330
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, will come, if you are making that effort, that you're gonna see it for yourself.

146
00:12:04,650 --> 00:12:04,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

147
00:12:06,202 --> 00:12:33,009
[SPEAKER_01]: By basically keeping an eye on my behavior, virtues, wholesome as well, like, you know, you might not be breaking the precepts, but you're engaging in pointless chit-chat, gossip, idle talk, you know, a bit of slander, a bit of a bad mouth in this, a bit of that, you know, occasionally seeking distractions, unnecessarily... Basically, you are still not restrained, as you should be, and you're maintaining these unwholesome themes in your mind, that then, you know,

148
00:12:33,589 --> 00:12:35,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You're literally keeping your mind wild to that extent.

149
00:12:36,372 --> 00:12:44,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when that wild mind decides to go its own way, starts criticizing, barking at you like a mad dog, well, you kept it mad.

150
00:12:44,421 --> 00:12:45,522
[SPEAKER_01]: You kept it untrained.

151
00:12:46,163 --> 00:12:49,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you have to endure its barking and trying to bite you every now and then.

152
00:12:50,147 --> 00:12:52,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it calms down, you better start training it.

153
00:12:54,736 --> 00:12:58,139
[SPEAKER_00]: You have developed that inner voice.

154
00:12:58,299 --> 00:12:58,999
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

155
00:12:59,239 --> 00:13:04,483
[SPEAKER_01]: There is... you were inadvertently feeding it and keeping it wild to that extent.

156
00:13:06,585 --> 00:13:15,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, this needs to be dealt with, the inner voice, the inner critic, the inner guilt-tripper, but it's actually the problem.

157
00:13:15,971 --> 00:13:17,632
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in that behavior.

158
00:13:17,712 --> 00:13:18,473
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the result.

159
00:13:19,382 --> 00:13:30,552
[SPEAKER_01]: of an underlying problem that I've been inadvertently not solving, such as you were keeping it wild through allowing it to uncontrollably go and do things and so on.

160
00:13:30,772 --> 00:13:39,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also something that, I mean, myself, I found that when, say, going into a very extreme solitude,

161
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:43,722
[SPEAKER_00]: You get to see that inner voice very quickly.

162
00:13:44,202 --> 00:13:44,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

163
00:13:44,982 --> 00:13:48,643
[SPEAKER_00]: When you've got no distractions, you get to see that inner voice very, very quickly.

164
00:13:49,183 --> 00:13:51,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And it takes a while... to calm down.

165
00:13:52,245 --> 00:13:52,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

166
00:13:53,085 --> 00:13:54,105
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's the difference there?

167
00:13:54,625 --> 00:13:57,847
[SPEAKER_01]: The difference is the environment of distraction.

168
00:13:59,107 --> 00:14:00,007
[SPEAKER_00]: There was none, so...

169
00:14:00,207 --> 00:14:00,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

170
00:14:00,588 --> 00:14:06,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, without a distraction, it becomes apparent now, because then... See, if a wild dog, or a semi-wild dog,

171
00:14:07,868 --> 00:14:09,890
[SPEAKER_01]: If it has its distractions, it won't bark at you.

172
00:14:11,211 --> 00:14:13,413
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not try to bite you and harass you.

173
00:14:13,953 --> 00:14:16,815
[SPEAKER_01]: You take away the distractions, it's gonna start complaining.

174
00:14:16,995 --> 00:14:19,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what the mind does when you take it into solitude.

175
00:14:21,139 --> 00:14:31,868
[SPEAKER_00]: But I also remember, also in my own experience, that going into solitude and then that inner voice becoming very apparent.

176
00:14:33,676 --> 00:14:34,956
[SPEAKER_00]: It was also very irritating.

177
00:14:34,976 --> 00:14:35,957
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what I mean?

178
00:14:35,977 --> 00:14:36,737
[SPEAKER_00]: You were averse to it.

179
00:14:36,757 --> 00:14:36,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

180
00:14:36,937 --> 00:14:37,637
[SPEAKER_00]: I was... Wow!

181
00:14:38,197 --> 00:14:38,618
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't...

182
00:14:38,938 --> 00:14:42,919
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't like this person and yet I'm with this person 24-7.

183
00:14:42,999 --> 00:14:43,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

184
00:14:44,299 --> 00:14:50,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're ignoring even your own aversion towards it because you were... you were being distracted through allowing distraction for it.

185
00:14:51,302 --> 00:14:51,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

186
00:14:51,902 --> 00:14:54,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, keeping yourself equally oblivious.

187
00:14:56,864 --> 00:14:57,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the important bit.

188
00:14:57,905 --> 00:15:05,833
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you get to see these things in solitude, and sooner or later you have to come out of solitude, and then realize, but I don't need to go back all the way to the distractions.

189
00:15:07,115 --> 00:15:19,247
[SPEAKER_01]: I can come out of the extreme solitude, or not necessarily live perpetually in the extreme solitude, but that's still not an excuse for me to now allow this animal to go back to its distractions and become wild again.

190
00:15:20,460 --> 00:15:21,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the work.

191
00:15:21,881 --> 00:15:22,502
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the training.

192
00:15:22,522 --> 00:15:23,442
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the guarding of the senses.

193
00:15:23,502 --> 00:15:30,708
[SPEAKER_01]: If the animal is tamed, and you protect it from the unwholesome, you know, the baits we spoke about, which is always your responsibility.

194
00:15:30,728 --> 00:15:32,029
[SPEAKER_01]: It's never... that never can...

195
00:15:32,669 --> 00:15:39,234
[SPEAKER_01]: The choices to accept the distraction, to accept lust, aversion, can never come from the environment.

196
00:15:39,274 --> 00:15:40,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Those choices are always yours.

197
00:15:41,215 --> 00:15:47,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Environment can provide you with hooks and baits, but you are the one always, every single time, responsible for choosing it.

198
00:15:49,031 --> 00:15:54,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you become aware of that inner voice, the more you practice solitude, and then you go out, you start to see that responsibility.

199
00:15:54,834 --> 00:16:03,297
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, yeah, I can actually... this is a good balance between solitude, like non-extreme solitude, and some degrees of solitude, and so on.

200
00:16:04,197 --> 00:16:11,580
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, okay, so I just... all I need to do is just protect this animal, this animal, not feed it distractions, lust, and aversion.

201
00:16:12,941 --> 00:16:17,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And then there is no basis for it to be barking at you and attacking you, because it's still calm.

202
00:16:18,827 --> 00:16:23,013
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not being agitated by things you allow it to agitate.

203
00:16:23,033 --> 00:16:25,075
[SPEAKER_00]: There's also...

204
00:16:25,256 --> 00:16:31,244
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, when I saw that the inner voice that I wasn't liking was a complaining voice.

205
00:16:32,225 --> 00:16:34,308
[SPEAKER_00]: And when everything... when there was no distraction...

206
00:16:36,475 --> 00:16:41,440
[SPEAKER_00]: That was clear that my mind was just complaining all the time about everything.

207
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:42,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was nothing to it.

208
00:16:43,342 --> 00:16:44,202
[SPEAKER_01]: There was nothing to care.

209
00:16:44,843 --> 00:16:45,263
[SPEAKER_00]: All the time.

210
00:16:45,344 --> 00:16:48,186
[SPEAKER_00]: But I never noticed how much I was doing that.

211
00:16:48,226 --> 00:16:48,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

212
00:16:49,387 --> 00:16:51,650
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it was just all the time complaining.

213
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:58,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, all of that, all of that energy behind all that complaining has been generated, cultivated.

214
00:16:58,076 --> 00:17:00,918
[SPEAKER_01]: The wildness of that animal has been maintained.

215
00:17:01,659 --> 00:17:27,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the choices of distraction, aversion, lust, you know, not even major sort of, you know, lust or desires or something, but still, on a day-to-day basis, you kept your mind, that animal, you were allowing it to be sort of distracted, dependent on the external sense objects, one way or the other, and when that's taken away, it was left with that aversion now, of complaining, attacking, barking, being a nuisance.

216
00:17:29,187 --> 00:17:35,993
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you didn't allow that animal to become distracted, there would be no basis for its complaining, because it can't calm down.

217
00:17:36,954 --> 00:17:49,785
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's in a critic, complaining voice, guilt-tripping voice, you realize that that's a symptom of the choices, themes that I cultivate, maintain, and I'm careless about throughout the day, when I'm not.

218
00:17:50,225 --> 00:17:51,986
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, when the mind is not complaining and so on.

219
00:17:52,006 --> 00:17:52,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

220
00:17:52,487 --> 00:17:58,311
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just basically become more aware of things you choose to, you know, do by body, by speech, by mind.

221
00:17:58,751 --> 00:18:03,254
[SPEAKER_01]: And really, as I said, it's not this external observance of be virtuous, be virtuous.

222
00:18:04,235 --> 00:18:16,424
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a direct connection between every single time you make this choice and how much, whether you, whether you fuel, whether you encourage animal, animal's wildness or whether you're taming it through your choices.

223
00:18:17,391 --> 00:18:22,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you adhere to them rightly, it won't take long before you realize, oh, it's actually tamed.

224
00:18:22,352 --> 00:18:23,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

225
00:18:23,252 --> 00:18:26,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Solitude is a good indicator.

226
00:18:26,053 --> 00:18:29,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Solitude reveals the symptoms, if there are any.

227
00:18:29,634 --> 00:18:30,894
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what I said.

228
00:18:31,194 --> 00:18:41,637
[SPEAKER_01]: If a monk doesn't develop... doesn't delight in solitude, doesn't, you know, make himself want solitude, he'll never understand the nature of his mind.

229
00:18:41,657 --> 00:18:43,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, why wouldn't he want solitude?

230
00:18:43,497 --> 00:18:44,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, exactly.

231
00:18:44,457 --> 00:18:47,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would a wild animal not want to become aware of its wildness?

232
00:18:47,339 --> 00:18:48,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's unpleasant.

233
00:18:48,079 --> 00:18:48,759
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant, yeah.

234
00:18:49,039 --> 00:18:54,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's dependent on distractions and solitude takes those away, so...

235
00:19:01,184 --> 00:19:05,366
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of that failure, again, some... another friend asked...

236
00:19:05,526 --> 00:19:08,407
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if we can say anything more on the subject, but...

237
00:19:10,513 --> 00:19:15,876
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of failure, when you feel like you've let others down.

238
00:19:15,896 --> 00:19:24,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say you fail your degree and feel miserable that you let your family down and see very little hope for the future.

239
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:30,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's quite concerned about or seems to be overwhelmed by letting others down.

240
00:19:32,163 --> 00:19:33,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, why is that a problem?

241
00:19:34,424 --> 00:19:34,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

242
00:19:35,865 --> 00:19:37,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because it's unpleasant.

243
00:19:39,427 --> 00:19:39,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

244
00:19:41,233 --> 00:19:46,714
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels unpleasant here and now, when others are disappointed with you, and that's why it's a problem for you.

245
00:19:47,675 --> 00:19:56,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, say you have a person you don't care about, you don't even know, and they just stop you on the street and say, I'm really disappointed in you.

246
00:19:58,497 --> 00:19:59,358
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't really care.

247
00:19:59,718 --> 00:20:04,239
[SPEAKER_01]: You might want to curiously find out why, but who is this person to be disappointed with me anyway?

248
00:20:05,099 --> 00:20:09,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So you want to recognize that the problem is, you don't really want the pain of it.

249
00:20:10,512 --> 00:20:15,037
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't want a pain of it, you should have been careful with your ambitions beforehand.

250
00:20:15,817 --> 00:20:23,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Because each time you want to achieve something, attain something, get something, there is a liability... you have to take responsibility for it.

251
00:20:23,445 --> 00:20:25,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you might not succeed to that extent.

252
00:20:26,580 --> 00:20:27,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not saying you shouldn't be ambitious.

253
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:31,281
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just saying don't take it for granted, but take responsibility for it.

254
00:20:31,361 --> 00:20:41,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you keep the possibility of failure in your mind, well, the disappointment of others will already be implicit in that, and it will not surprise you or overwhelm you.

255
00:20:41,824 --> 00:20:45,105
[SPEAKER_01]: So, given that now you're overwhelmed, pretty much you have to endure the pain.

256
00:20:45,165 --> 00:20:46,245
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't... it won't last forever.

257
00:20:46,946 --> 00:20:48,046
[SPEAKER_01]: When the pain fades away,

258
00:20:48,932 --> 00:20:50,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Reconsider your ambition.

259
00:20:50,273 --> 00:20:52,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Find something else if you want to be ambitious about and do it.

260
00:20:53,114 --> 00:20:59,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't just sort of blindly commit to it and forget that, yeah, you might not succeed.

261
00:20:59,557 --> 00:21:04,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And, and, you know, also, fundamentally, others can always find reasons to be disappointed with you.

262
00:21:05,560 --> 00:21:07,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's another thing perhaps you should re-evaluate.

263
00:21:08,561 --> 00:21:09,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasing others.

264
00:21:10,424 --> 00:21:15,065
[SPEAKER_01]: How far you want to go with that, because, you know, if you start going that way, it is endless.

265
00:21:15,705 --> 00:21:19,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You can never please others, because they would always have opinions and views and everything.

266
00:21:19,946 --> 00:21:36,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sure, you don't ignore, you know, others in that sense, but especially, you know, family, people you live with, but at the same time, the point will come where you do need to draw some sort of line for yourself and for others, as like, well, yeah, but this is my life and my choices, my responsibility, my results.

267
00:21:37,957 --> 00:21:42,561
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, accept, you know, the nagging, or being upset, or whatever, as part of that.

268
00:21:42,681 --> 00:21:44,602
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to have ill will on account of that either.

269
00:21:46,484 --> 00:21:53,529
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are not clear with yourself, well, you will not be clear in regard to where that line is, and then...

270
00:21:53,549 --> 00:21:56,412
[SPEAKER_00]: When people praise you, or blame you, you just... Well, exactly.

271
00:21:56,792 --> 00:22:00,635
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not be able to sort of withstand it without losing perspective.

272
00:22:01,215 --> 00:22:04,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, now it feels like, oh, it's all in there or something, but it's not.

273
00:22:05,515 --> 00:22:13,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure that person has experienced this, others being disappointed with them before, in regard to something, something else.

274
00:22:13,961 --> 00:22:16,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And it came, and it went.

275
00:22:16,583 --> 00:22:23,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to completely dismiss it, but you certainly shouldn't be like, oh, I must be pleasing others, because even that, it's rooted just because you don't want to suffer.

276
00:22:24,448 --> 00:22:31,873
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when you feel like, I must please others, it's because it's too unpleasant for me, when others are displeased for me, eh, with me.

277
00:22:33,248 --> 00:22:36,050
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, that's impossible to achieve.

278
00:22:38,692 --> 00:22:45,416
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think you can avoid the pain by preventing or providing others with what they want,

279
00:22:46,878 --> 00:22:47,739
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just gonna suffer more.

280
00:22:48,859 --> 00:22:56,244
[SPEAKER_00]: As the, in that one sutta, the Buddha says it, nobody is fully blamed, fully praised.

281
00:22:56,284 --> 00:22:59,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody is praised all the time, or blamed all the time.

282
00:22:59,726 --> 00:23:04,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Even Tathāgata was blamed, and even the voice people are sometimes praised.

283
00:23:05,549 --> 00:23:10,312
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to accept that, and accept that really the issue with that is like, again, you don't lose perspective.

284
00:23:10,332 --> 00:23:11,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I failed.

285
00:23:12,160 --> 00:23:13,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I could have done better.

286
00:23:13,601 --> 00:23:14,641
[SPEAKER_01]: I should try and do better.

287
00:23:15,341 --> 00:23:20,523
[SPEAKER_01]: But, don't become irrational about it in a sense of, oh, but, but they're so disappointed, worried, worried.

288
00:23:20,723 --> 00:23:22,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's actually not about others.

289
00:23:22,644 --> 00:23:25,105
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about you not wanting the unpleasantness of it.

290
00:23:26,065 --> 00:23:31,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you think by managing and providing others what they want, you can avoid this dukkha that you have to endure.

291
00:23:31,087 --> 00:23:31,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no.

292
00:23:31,528 --> 00:23:37,170
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to avoid the dukkha that you can endure, is... that you are enduring, is by enduring it and taking responsibility for it.

293
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:42,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And not trying to manage the external world in account to get rid of that which you're averse to.

294
00:23:43,134 --> 00:23:44,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's really the problem.

295
00:23:44,855 --> 00:23:50,599
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem of others being disappointed, the problem of others blaming you or something, is that just feels painful.

296
00:23:51,540 --> 00:23:53,241
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't want to deal with that pain.

297
00:23:55,223 --> 00:23:57,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they have a point, sometimes they don't.

298
00:23:58,265 --> 00:24:03,469
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you want to maintain that perspective, you have to accept what you're feeling on account of others, not try to get rid of it.

299
00:24:04,310 --> 00:24:07,332
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, by accepting the pain, you will also not have ill will towards them.

300
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:12,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not trying to get rid of dukkha, except the responsibility for it.

301
00:24:13,202 --> 00:24:16,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You're enduring it, you're seeing it for what it is, so there's no aversion towards it.

302
00:24:17,424 --> 00:24:22,306
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get to see what they're complaining, what they're upset, what they're disappointed about, and it's like, actually, yeah, they have a point.

303
00:24:23,609 --> 00:24:27,250
[SPEAKER_01]: And from that perspective, you will get to actually act accordingly and upgrade your behavior.

304
00:24:28,011 --> 00:24:30,812
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, sometimes you'll see, no, they're completely irrational.

305
00:24:31,592 --> 00:24:33,353
[SPEAKER_01]: They just want... they just want what they want.

306
00:24:33,693 --> 00:24:34,953
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't really even care about this.

307
00:24:34,993 --> 00:24:35,953
[SPEAKER_01]: They just want what they want.

308
00:24:36,434 --> 00:24:38,855
[SPEAKER_01]: And in that case, you will not entertain that and feed that further.

309
00:24:40,035 --> 00:24:45,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll not be angry about it either, but you will certainly be able to remain unmovable.

310
00:24:47,137 --> 00:24:51,199
[SPEAKER_00]: How to cultivate a sense of urgency as a lay person?

311
00:24:53,559 --> 00:24:59,964
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be a practical approach to find motivation to make dhamma practice a priority as a lay person?

312
00:25:01,566 --> 00:25:04,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, incessant reflection on the nature of your situation.

313
00:25:05,369 --> 00:25:06,369
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to make the effort.

314
00:25:06,850 --> 00:25:11,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't think about it, the mind forgets, drifts away, gets pulled with sensuality, you lose sense of urgency.

315
00:25:12,474 --> 00:25:16,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have to think about things that you are subjected to in that world.

316
00:25:17,393 --> 00:25:23,277
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes it might be like a little prompt, sometimes you might just have to sit and dwell on everything that could go wrong.

317
00:25:24,258 --> 00:25:26,339
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will bring you with anxiety and sense of urgency.

318
00:25:26,919 --> 00:25:29,321
[SPEAKER_01]: If you already have it, you don't need to dwell on these things.

319
00:25:29,401 --> 00:25:39,728
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want that kind of a bit of a sharpness to it, because otherwise you can't move towards the direction of dhamma at all, sensuality is too overwhelming, then yeah,

320
00:25:41,450 --> 00:25:43,651
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure things would have happened to everyone in their life.

321
00:25:43,711 --> 00:25:44,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that they don't want to think about.

322
00:25:46,011 --> 00:25:46,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So, think about that.

323
00:25:47,632 --> 00:25:48,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can happen again.

324
00:25:48,892 --> 00:25:50,073
[SPEAKER_00]: I think about the fact that...

325
00:25:50,413 --> 00:25:51,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Liability to suffering.

326
00:25:51,753 --> 00:25:55,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Your priority is sensuality and not dhamma.

327
00:25:55,535 --> 00:25:56,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And you... Yeah.

328
00:25:56,495 --> 00:25:57,435
[SPEAKER_00]: You're struggling to get out.

329
00:25:57,455 --> 00:25:58,476
[SPEAKER_00]: You're completely trapped.

330
00:25:59,436 --> 00:26:05,258
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, like when sensuality comes, they would not necessarily feel the sense of urgency to restrain themselves.

331
00:26:05,478 --> 00:26:06,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to dwell on it.

332
00:26:06,518 --> 00:26:08,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to sustain that context that will keep things urgent.

333
00:26:11,479 --> 00:26:19,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, always whoever asks that question, they recognize that when they lose the urgency, they kind of go back to sensuality and lose the practice.

334
00:26:20,207 --> 00:26:21,648
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, that is not uncommon.

335
00:26:22,549 --> 00:26:33,819
[SPEAKER_01]: And even in the suttas, the Buddha would basically scare monks, light the fire under them, by reminding them what's still there, so they get the sense of urgency, because they still have work to do.

336
00:26:35,248 --> 00:26:36,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they just got negligent.

337
00:26:37,090 --> 00:26:40,673
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to find something and then, yeah, cultivate it.

338
00:26:40,753 --> 00:26:42,234
[SPEAKER_01]: When it runs out, find another thing.

339
00:26:42,875 --> 00:26:48,280
[SPEAKER_01]: They will bring a bit of anxiety and sense of urgency, so that you can actually sustain your precepts and practice towards dhamma.

340
00:26:51,423 --> 00:26:52,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't wait till it's too late.

341
00:26:52,504 --> 00:26:54,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't wait until the world actually throws something at you.

342
00:26:55,346 --> 00:26:55,767
[SPEAKER_01]: They will.

343
00:26:57,233 --> 00:26:59,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Fill you with anxiety and fear and be too much to handle.

344
00:27:00,256 --> 00:27:05,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start preparing yourself and training yourself for it already.

345
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:06,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can, as I said.

346
00:27:07,602 --> 00:27:10,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Just choose the right themes and it will arise.

347
00:27:12,794 --> 00:27:30,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Sickness, aging, death, you know, any basically form of dukkha, discomfort, suffering you experienced, anything you fear you might, you would experience, anything you don't experience and you don't think about it, all of that is all revealing that you're still liable to these things, you're still liable to be affected by it.

348
00:27:30,888 --> 00:27:34,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about it and you will feel, oh, I need to address this urgently.

349
00:27:35,372 --> 00:27:37,934
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be a natural outcome.

